        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keep it under 1,500 PSI, use the 40 degree white nozzle, and stand two to three feet back. Distance is a finer pressure control than the machine's own setting, and sound flat paint is far tougher than people fear. What is genuinely vulnerable is edges: stone chips, panel shuts, badge adhesive, peeling lacquer and wrap edges, where the stream gets underneath.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Treat the machine as a rinsing and foaming tool rather than a washing one. Pre-rinse, apply foam bottom to top with a cannon, let it dwell for several minutes, then rinse top to bottom. That sequence removes the grit that a mitt would otherwise drag across the paint, which is the actual cause of swirl marks.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then finish properly. Two buckets with grit guards and separate kit for wheels, no dish soap, and dry promptly in the shade before minerals etch into the clear coat. And in a salted climate, remember that the most valuable thing this machine does for a car is a low-pressure rinse of the underside that nobody will ever see.
          </p>
        </div>


